  <h2>What coming back on feels like</h2>

  <p>Restarting was both a relief and a defeat. The relief was immediate — within two weeks on 0.5mg, the food noise quieted again. Not as dramatically as the first time, but enough. The cravings softened. The constant mental negotiation about food faded into background noise. I could think about other things again.</p>

  <p>The defeat took longer to process. I'd wanted so badly to be someone who could manage without medication. Going back felt like giving in. My therapist (the same one I'd been seeing for other reasons) reframed it in a way that helped: "You wouldn't stop taking blood pressure medication because your blood pressure improved. The improvement <em>is</em> the medication working."</p>

  <p>She was right. But knowing that intellectually and accepting it emotionally are two different things.</p>

  <h2>The maintenance dose approach</h2>

  <p>I've been back on Ozempic for three months now, at 0.5mg instead of the 1.0mg I was on before. The experience is different this time:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Appetite suppression is milder.</strong> I'm not as aggressively appetite-suppressed as I was on 1.0mg. I eat more than I did at peak Ozempic, but substantially less than I was eating off it. It feels sustainable rather than extreme.</li>
    <li><strong>Weight loss is slower.</strong> I've lost about 13 lbs in three months back on — much slower than my initial round. My doctor says this is normal for restart and for a lower dose. The goal now is stabilisation, not rapid loss.</li>
    <li><strong>Side effects are minimal.</strong> Barely any nausea this time around. Whether that's the lower dose or my body remembering the medication, I don't know. But restarting was physically easy.</li>
    <li><strong>My mindset is different.</strong> I'm not planning to stop again. That's the biggest change. I've accepted that this is likely a long-term medication for me, not a temporary intervention. That acceptance has actually been freeing — I've stopped viewing it as something I need to escape from.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I wish someone had told me</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The habits aren't yours — they're the medication's.</strong> The portion control, the reduced cravings, the quiet food noise — those feel like personal achievements while you're on the medication. They're not. They're pharmacological effects. When the medication goes, they go with it. The exceptions to this are rare.</li>
    <li><strong>The "training wheels" metaphor is wrong.</strong> GLP-1 medications don't teach your brain new habits that persist after stopping. They change your brain chemistry while you're taking them. A better metaphor is glasses: they correct your vision while you're wearing them, but your eyes don't improve when you take them off.</li>
    <li><strong>Your doctor's caution isn't pessimism.</strong> When my doctor suggested a maintenance dose instead of stopping, she wasn't being negative. She was reading the data accurately. Clinical trials consistently show that two-thirds of weight lost on GLP-1 medications is regained within a year of stopping. I thought I'd be in the other third. I wasn't.</li>
    <li><strong>Regain is not failure.</strong> This was the hardest thing to accept. Regaining weight after stopping a GLP-1 isn't a character flaw or a lack of willpower. It's the predicted biological outcome of removing a medication that was treating an ongoing condition. Obesity is chronic. Treating it with a medication and then stopping is like treating hypertension with a pill and then wondering why your blood pressure went back up.</li>
    <li><strong>A maintenance dose is a legitimate strategy.</strong> You don't have to choose between full-dose treatment and nothing. Many doctors are now prescribing lower maintenance doses for patients who've reached their goals. This is emerging as a sensible long-term approach — enough medication to maintain the benefits without the intensity (or cost) of the full treatment dose.</li>
    <li><strong>Going back on isn't starting over.</strong> Restarting felt like failure at first. It wasn't. I still have the foundation from my first 14 months. The restart is faster and easier. Going back is not the same as going back to square one.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>If you're thinking about stopping your GLP-1 medication:</strong> Talk to your doctor first. Ask about stepping down to a maintenance dose rather than stopping entirely. Have a realistic conversation about the likelihood of regain. If you do decide to stop, monitor your weight closely in the first few months — if it starts trending upward, that's the time to intervene, not after you've regained 30 lbs. Early action is everything.
  </div>

  <h2>Where I am now</h2>

  <p>I'm at 183 lbs — down from 196 when I restarted, still above my lowest of 165. I may never get back to 165, and I'm making peace with that. My doctor says a stable weight in the mid-170s on a maintenance dose would be an excellent outcome. I'm inclined to agree.</p>

  <p>The biggest change isn't physical. It's philosophical. I've stopped thinking of Ozempic as a temporary fix and started thinking of it as ongoing treatment for a chronic condition. That shift in framing has removed the pressure to "graduate" from the medication, and with it, most of the shame.</p>

  <p>I'm not the exception I wanted to be. But I'm managing my health, I'm maintaining most of the weight I lost, and I'm doing it without the constant guilt of feeling like I should be able to do this on my own. That's enough. It's more than enough.</p>

